cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "spherical-ramsey: which finite spherical sets are Euclidean Ramsey (Lean 4 ropes, exact certificates, probes, note and conjecture ledger)"
version: "1.1.1"
date-released: "2026-10-15"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23366343"
authors:
  - family-names: "Strinz"
    given-names: "Will"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/wstrinz/spherical-ramsey"
abstract: >-
  Lean 4 proofs, exact certificates and numerical probes about which finite sets
  of points on a sphere are Euclidean Ramsey, building on OpenAI's classification
  of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations (openai/math, result 172) and its Lean
  proof, which is trusted. Every result carries its verification level: LEAN
  (checked by stock Comparator, standard axioms only), CERT (an exact symbolic
  identity, determinant or certificate checked by a script, plus a written
  argument), HAND (a written proof, reviewed by AI only) or PROBE (evidence, not
  proof). Five Lean ropes, standalone packages each checked by Comparator on a
  fresh runner: finite spherical sets with algebraic coordinates are Ramsey (prior
  work; a further proof); any six or fewer points on a circle are Ramsey; the
  radian nine-gon (nine distinct points) is Ramsey if and only if e^{i theta} is
  algebraic; the Ramsey property of a set on the unit sphere is invariant under
  Moebius maps (Theorem C, published in mobius-ramsey; other spheres by a
  similarity, by hand); algebraic concyclic points plus at most three
  arbitrary points are Ramsey. The two-group theorem: a union of two Moebius
  images of finite algebraic subsets of the sphere S^{d-1} is Ramsey for every
  d >= 2 (HAND, AI-reviewed), with exact certificates on S^1 to S^5 (CERT). Exact
  certificates also give cyclic Ramsey sets that are not unions of two Moebius
  images of algebraic sets (CERT plus glue, reviewed by AI), and non-Ramsey
  configurations on S^2 and S^3. A note states each claim at its level, and a
  conjecture ledger keeps the open and refuted conjectures with replay commands. Version 1.1.0 corrects the credit of
  version 1.0.0: OpenAI's quadric-independence criterion (result 172,
  Proposition 7.3) and Palvolgyi's appendix Theorems A.11-A.13 had been
  overlooked; several conjecture-ledger statuses change accordingly. It adds
  ledger entries SR-C29 to SR-C35 (among them first-order rigidity, whose argument
  was reviewed by one AI agent) and a frontier document. Version 1.1.1 adds to
  the note the written proofs of first-order rigidity (SR-C34) and of the
  finite-type reformulation (SR-C33(a)-(c)), which v1.1.0 labelled HAND while
  publishing only a summary of the argument (a second, independent AI review of
  the written proofs found every step sound), and scopes Theorem C's LEAN level
  to the unit sphere.
  AI disclosure. The author, Will Strinz, is the only human contributor. The
  proofs, the Lean, the certificates, the probes, the checks and the documentation
  were produced by AI agents (Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Sonnet 5.5, Anthropic, in
  Claude Code) under his direction. Every review reported here is an AI review,
  except the author's own read; no human expert has reviewed this work. See
  DISCLOSURE.md.
  Licence. A mixed tree: this project's own files are under the MIT License; the
  vendored and adapted OpenAI Lean files under ropes/ are under the Apache License
  2.0. NOTICE lists which file is under which licence.
keywords:
  - Euclidean Ramsey theory
  - spherical configurations
  - Moebius transformations
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
  - exact certificates
  - Mathlib
